    "focus": "<body>\n  <a class=\"skip-link\" href=\"#main\">Skip to lesson</a>\n  <header class=\"topbar\">\n    <a class=\"brand\" href=\"#\" aria-label=\"RecallWeave home\"><span class=\"brand-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\">r</span> recall<span>weave</span></a>\n    <div class=\"local-badge\"><span aria-hidden=\"true\">●</span> PRIVATE · RUNS ON THIS DEVICE</div>\n  </header>\n  <main id=\"main\" class=\"layout\">\n    <section class=\"intro\" aria-labelledby=\"page-title\">\n      <div class=\"eyebrow\">FIELD NOTES  /  BIOLOGY 01</div>\n      <h1 id=\"page-title\">Learn the <em>links,</em><br>not just the labels.</h1>\n      <p>Build a mental model of how cells move energy—from sunlight to usable work. Each next prompt is chosen from your learning state, not a fixed quiz order.</p>\n      <div class=\"lesson-meta\"><span class=\"meta-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span><span>6 short challenges</span><span class=\"dot\">·</span><span>about 4 minutes</span></div>\n      <div class=\"concept-map\" aria-label=\"Concept pathway: light energy to glucose to ATP\">\n        <div class=\"map-node active\"><span>01</span>Light energy</div><div class=\"map-line\"></div><div class=\"map-node\"><span>02</span>Glucose</div><div class=\"map-line\"></div><div class=\"map-node\"><span>03</span>ATP</div>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"map-caption\">A concept pathway. The selector can revisit a prerequisite when evidence suggests a useful review.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class=\"workbench\" aria-label=\"Interactive learning session\">\n      <div class=\"progress-row\"><div class=\"progress-label\"><span id=\"step-label\">PRACTICE ROUND</span><span id=\"step-count\">1 / 6</span></div><div class=\"progress-track\" role=\"progressbar\" aria-label=\"Practice progress\" aria-valuemin=\"0\" aria-valuemax=\"6\" aria-valuenow=\"1\"><span id=\"progress-fill\" style=\"width: 16.6667%;\"></span></div></div>\n      <div id=\"session-content\" aria-live=\"polite\"><article class=\"question-card practice-card\"><div class=\"question-type\">PRACTICE 1 OF 6 · LIGHT CAPTURE</div><h2>A plant is kept in light but cannot make a key photosynthetic pigment. Which first-order change is most likely?</h2><p class=\"prompt\">Recall the explanation. Choose the best answer once more.</p><p class=\"practice-note\">This retry is recorded separately from your first try and model estimates.</p><div class=\"choices\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Practice answer options\"><button class=\"choice correct\" data-practice-choice=\"0\" disabled=\"\"><span class=\"choice-key\">A</span>Less light energy is captured for the light reactions</button><button class=\"choice\" data-practice-choice=\"1\" disabled=\"\"><span class=\"choice-key\">B</span>More ATP is made directly from stored glucose</button><button class=\"choice incorrect\" data-practice-choice=\"2\" disabled=\"\"><span class=\"choice-key\">C</span>Carbon dioxide becomes the main energy source</button><button class=\"choice\" data-practice-choice=\"3\" disabled=\"\"><span class=\"choice-key\">D</span>Oxygen is consumed to split water</button></div><div id=\"practice-feedback\"><div class=\"feedback\"><strong>Keep this connection in view.</strong><p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> Less light energy is captured for the light reactions</p>Photosynthetic pigments absorb light energy that drives the light-dependent reactions. Blocking pigment function reduces that energy capture.<span class=\"why\">Apply the idea: This links a visible input (light) to the first energy-conversion step.</span></div><button class=\"primary-button next-button\" id=\"practice-next\">Next practice connection <span aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></button></div><button class=\"text-button practice-back\" id=\"back-to-review\">Back to learning trace</button></article></div>\n      <details class=\"model-note\"><summary>How the adaptive model works</summary><p>Bayesian Knowledge Tracing estimates mastery per concept using explicit initial-knowledge, guess, slip, and learning-transition probabilities. After each response, the selector scores unasked items by expected information gain, weighted toward prerequisite concepts. The values are illustrative defaults, not a validated assessment.</p></details>\n    </section>\n  </main>\n  <footer><span>RECALLWEAVE · DEMO BUILD</span><span>Original demo deck · OpenStax Biology 2e attribution in the wrap-up</span></footer>\n<script id=\"deck-json\" type=\"application/json\">{\n  \"title\": \"Energy in cells: from light to ATP\",\n  \"license\": \"CC BY 4.0 for source material; all question wording and distractors in this demo are original, adapted from the cited open textbook concepts.\",\n  \"attribution\": \"OpenStax, Biology 2e, Chapters 7–8 (Cellular Respiration and Photosynthesis), Rice University, CC BY 4.0. https://openstax.org/details/books/biology-2e\",\n  \"concepts\": [\"photosynthesis\", \"glucose\", \"cellular-respiration\", \"atp\"],\n  \"items\": [\n    {\"id\":\"p1\",\"concept\":\"photosynthesis\",\"prerequisites\":[],\"prompt\":\"A plant is kept in light but cannot make a key photosynthetic pigment. Which first-order change is most likely?\",\"options\":[\"Less light energy is captured for the light reactions\",\"More ATP is made directly from stored glucose\",\"Carbon dioxide becomes the main energy source\",\"Oxygen is consumed to split water\"],\"answer\":0,\"explanation\":\"Photosynthetic pigments absorb light energy that drives the light-dependent reactions. Blocking pigment function reduces that energy capture.\",\"transfer\":\"This links a visible input (light) to the first energy-conversion step.\"},\n    {\"id\":\"p2\",\"concept\":\"photosynthesis\",\"prerequisites\":[],\"prompt\":\"Which statement best describes the role of the Calvin cycle?\",\"options\":[\"It uses ATP and NADPH to help build carbohydrate from carbon dioxide\",\"It splits glucose to release energy for muscle contraction\",\"It captures light directly to make oxygen\",\"It transfers electrons from food to oxygen\"],\"answer\":0,\"explanation\":\"The Calvin cycle uses energy carriers produced by the light reactions to fix carbon dioxide into carbohydrate building blocks; it does not capture light directly.\",\"transfer\":\"Keep the two linked stages distinct: light capture supplies chemical energy; carbon fixation uses it.\"},\n    {\"id\":\"g1\",\"concept\":\"glucose\",\"prerequisites\":[\"photosynthesis\"],\"prompt\":\"A learner says, “Plants get their usable energy from soil.” Which correction best connects photosynthesis to stored chemical energy?\",\"options\":[\"Light energy can be stored in energy-rich molecules such as sugars made from carbon dioxide\",\"Minerals in soil are converted directly into ATP\",\"Plants absorb glucose through roots as their only fuel\",\"Soil provides the electrons that power the Calvin cycle\"],\"answer\":0,\"explanation\":\"Photosynthesis uses light energy to build energy-rich carbohydrates from carbon dioxide (with water involved in the overall process). Soil minerals support growth but are not the energy source for sugar synthesis.\",\"transfer\":\"Explain the energy path: light → chemical energy in sugars; nutrients and energy are different things.\"},\n    {\"id\":\"r1\",\"concept\":\"cellular-respiration\",\"prerequisites\":[\"glucose\"],\"prompt\":\"A cell has glucose available but its oxygen supply drops sharply. What is the strongest prediction for aerobic cellular respiration?\",\"options\":[\"The electron transport chain is constrained because oxygen is its terminal electron acceptor\",\"Photosynthesis starts inside the mitochondrion\",\"Glucose can no longer enter any metabolic pathway\",\"The Calvin cycle speeds up to make ATP\"],\"answer\":0,\"explanation\":\"In aerobic respiration, oxygen accepts electrons at the end of the mitochondrial electron transport chain. Limited oxygen constrains that pathway; cells may use other processes, but those are not the same as aerobic respiration.\",\"transfer\":\"The stored energy in glucose is released through linked pathways; oxygen's role is at the chain's end.\"},\n    {\"id\":\"a1\",\"concept\":\"atp\",\"prerequisites\":[\"cellular-respiration\"],\"prompt\":\"Why is ATP useful as an immediate energy carrier even though cells store much more energy in fuel molecules?\",\"options\":[\"Its hydrolysis can be coupled to cellular work in manageable transfers\",\"It permanently stores all energy without being recycled\",\"It is the main molecule that captures sunlight\",\"It contains more total energy than a glucose molecule\"],\"answer\":0,\"explanation\":\"ATP hydrolysis can be coupled to endergonic cellular processes. Cells continually regenerate ATP; it is an immediate transferable energy currency, not a long-term bulk store.\",\"transfer\":\"Distinguish the fuel reservoir (for example, glucose) from the frequently regenerated spendable carrier (ATP).\"},\n    {\"id\":\"x1\",\"concept\":\"atp\",\"prerequisites\":[\"photosynthesis\",\"cellular-respiration\"],\"prompt\":\"Which comparison best connects photosynthesis and cellular respiration?\",\"options\":[\"They are complementary energy transformations: photosynthesis stores energy in organic molecules, while respiration transfers energy from those molecules to ATP\",\"They are the same pathway running in opposite directions in the same organelle\",\"Photosynthesis consumes ATP and respiration makes sunlight\",\"Both processes only occur in plants\"],\"answer\":0,\"explanation\":\"Photosynthesis stores captured light energy in organic molecules; cellular respiration transfers energy from organic molecules into ATP. The pathways are connected but are not simply identical reverse reactions.\",\"transfer\":\"Use the model to explain why food webs depend on photosynthetic energy capture and why cells need respiration.\"}\n  ]\n}\n</script>\n<script>\n/**\n * Transparent Bayesian Knowledge Tracing and adaptive item selection.\n * Parameters are pedagogical defaults, not fitted or validated estimates.\n * @typedef {{id:string, concept:string, prerequisites:string[], options:string[], answer:number, prompt:string, explanation:string, transfer:string}} Item\n * @typedef {{initial:number, learn:number, guess:number, slip:number}} BktParameters\n */\nconst DEFAULT_BKT = Object.freeze({ initial: 0.22, learn: 0.18, guess: 0.2, slip: 0.1 });\nconst clamp = (value, min = 0, max = 1) => Math.min(max, Math.max(min, value));\n\n/** Bernoulli entropy in bits. @param {number} p */\nfunction entropy(p) {\n  const q = clamp(p);\n  if (q === 0 || q === 1) return 0;\n  return -q * Math.log2(q) - (1 - q) * Math.log2(1 - q);\n}\n\n/**\n * One BKT update. Correctness is evidence, not certainty; a guess and a slip remain possible.\n * @param {number} prior\n * @param {boolean} correct\n * @param {BktParameters} parameters\n */\nfunction updateMastery(prior, correct, parameters = DEFAULT_BKT) {\n  const p = clamp(prior);\n  const { guess, slip, learn } = parameters;\n  for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(parameters)) {\n    if (!Number.isFinite(value) || value < 0 || value > 1) throw new RangeError(`${key} must be in [0,1]`);\n  }\n  const pCorrect = p * (1 - slip) + (1 - p) * guess;\n  const posterior = correct ? p * (1 - slip) / pCorrect : p * slip / (p * slip + (1 - p) * (1 - guess));\n  return clamp(posterior + (1 - posterior) * learn);\n}\n\n/** Expected reduction in uncertainty from an item response under its concept mastery.\n * @param {number} mastery\n * @param {BktParameters} parameters\n */\nfunction expectedInformationGain(mastery, parameters = DEFAULT_BKT) {\n  const p = clamp(mastery);\n  const { guess, slip } = parameters;\n  const pCorrect = p * (1 - slip) + (1 - p) * guess;\n  const correctPosterior = p * (1 - slip) / pCorrect;\n  const wrongPosterior = p * slip / (p * slip + (1 - p) * (1 - guess));\n  return Math.max(0, entropy(p) - pCorrect * entropy(correctPosterior) - (1 - pCorrect) * entropy(wrongPosterior));\n}\n\n/**\n * Select an unseen item maximizing uncertainty reduction plus prerequisite repair.\n * Tie-break is stable by item id, making the choice reproducible.\n * @param {Item[]} items\n * @param {Set<string>} asked\n * @param {Record<string,number>} mastery\n * @param {BktParameters} parameters\n * @returns {Item|null}\n */\nfunction selectNextItem(items, asked, mastery, parameters = DEFAULT_BKT) {\n  const candidates = items.filter(item => !asked.has(item.id));\n  if (!candidates.length) return null;\n  const score = item => {\n    const m = mastery[item.concept] ?? parameters.initial;\n    // Prefer a weak concept that unlocks several still-unanswered downstream ideas.\n    const downstream = candidates.filter(next => next.id !== item.id && next.prerequisites.includes(item.concept)).length;\n    const prerequisiteRepair = (1 - m) * Math.min(3, downstream) * 0.12;\n    return expectedInformationGain(m, parameters) + prerequisiteRepair;\n  };\n  return candidates.sort((a, b) => score(b) - score(a) || a.id.localeCompare(b.id))[0];\n}\n\n/** Clone session probabilities so learners' local run has no shared state. */\nfunction initialMastery(concepts, parameters = DEFAULT_BKT) {\n  return Object.fromEntries(concepts.map(concept => [concept, parameters.initial]));\n}\n\n/**\n * Reproducible toy learner run; simulated evidence is never real learner data.\n * @param {Item[]} items\n * @param {string[]} concepts\n * @param {Record<string,number>} latentKnowledge simulated probability of knowing each concept\n * @param {boolean} adaptive use the information-gain selector, or fixed deck order\n * @param {number} seed deterministic pseudo-random seed\n */\nfunction runLearnerSimulation(items, concepts, latentKnowledge, adaptive = true, seed = 20261005) {\n  let state = seed >>> 0 || 1;\n  const random = () => {\n    state ^= state << 13; state ^= state >>> 17; state ^= state << 5;\n    return (state >>> 0) / 4294967296;\n  };\n  const stateByConcept = initialMastery(concepts);\n  const asked = new Set();\n  const trace = [];\n  while (asked.size < items.length) {\n    const item = adaptive ? selectNextItem(items, asked, stateByConcept) : items.find(candidate => !asked.has(candidate.id));\n    const knowledge = clamp(latentKnowledge[item.concept] ?? 0.5);\n    const correct = random() < 0.18 + knowledge * 0.72;\n    asked.add(item.id);\n    stateByConcept[item.concept] = updateMastery(stateByConcept[item.concept], correct);\n    trace.push({ item: item.id, concept: item.concept, correct });\n  }\n  const meanEstimatedMastery = concepts.reduce((sum, concept) => sum + stateByConcept[concept], 0) / concepts.length;\n  return { trace, mastery: stateByConcept, meanEstimatedMastery };\n}\n\n/**\n * A local review snapshot and one bounded practice round.\n * Practice never updates the original answers or the knowledge model.\n */\n\nfunction validChoice(item, choice) {\n  if (!Number.isInteger(choice) || choice < 0 || choice >= item.options.length) {\n    throw new RangeError('Choose one of this question’s answer options.');\n  }\n}\n\n/** Keep the first-attempt order, chosen answer and explanation available for review. */\nfunction createReview(items, answers) {\n  const byId = new Map(items.map(item => [item.id, item]));\n  const seen = new Set();\n  return Object.freeze(answers.map(answer => {\n    const item = byId.get(answer.item);\n    if (!item || seen.has(item.id)) throw new RangeError('Review needs one first answer per known question.');\n    seen.add(item.id);\n    validChoice(item, answer.choice);\n    return Object.freeze({\n      id: item.id,\n      concept: item.concept,\n      prompt: item.prompt,\n      options: Object.freeze([...item.options]),\n      answer: item.answer,\n      choice: answer.choice,\n      correct: answer.choice === item.answer,\n      explanation: item.explanation,\n      transfer: item.transfer\n    });\n  }));\n}\n\n/** Each initially missed question gets one retry, in its original session order. */\nfunction beginPractice(review) {\n  return Object.freeze({\n    items: Object.freeze(review.filter(item => !item.correct)),\n    answers: Object.freeze([])\n  });\n}\n\nfunction currentPracticeItem(round) {\n  return round.items[round.answers.length] ?? null;\n}\n\n/** Return new practice state; both the review and earlier round states stay intact. */\nfunction answerPractice(round, itemId, choice) {\n  const item = currentPracticeItem(round);\n  if (!item || item.id !== itemId) throw new RangeError('Only the current unanswered practice question can be answered.');\n  validChoice(item, choice);\n  return Object.freeze({\n    items: round.items,\n    answers: Object.freeze([...round.answers, Object.freeze({item: item.id, choice, correct: choice === item.answer})])\n  });\n}\n\nconst root = document.querySelector('#session-content');\nconst deck = JSON.parse(document.querySelector('#deck-json').textContent);\nconst concepts = deck.concepts;\nconst mastery = initialMastery(concepts);\nconst asked = new Set();\nconst answers = [];\nlet review = null;\nlet practice = null;\nlet inPractice = false;\nconst progressFill = document.querySelector('#progress-fill');\nconst progressTrack = document.querySelector('[role=\"progressbar\"]');\nconst stepLabel = document.querySelector('#step-label');\nconst stepCount = document.querySelector('#step-count');\n\nfunction setProgress() {\n  const count = inPractice ? practice.answers.length : answers.length;\n  const total = inPractice ? practice.items.length : deck.items.length;\n  stepCount.textContent = `${count} / ${total}`;\n  progressFill.style.width = `${total ? count / total * 100 : 0}%`;\n  progressTrack.setAttribute('aria-label', inPractice ? 'Practice progress' : 'Lesson progress');\n  progressTrack.setAttribute('aria-valuemax', String(total));\n  progressTrack.setAttribute('aria-valuenow', String(count));\n  stepLabel.textContent = inPractice\n    ? (count === total ? 'PRACTICE COMPLETE' : 'PRACTICE ROUND')\n    : count === total ? 'THREAD COMPLETE' : count ? 'FOLLOW THE CONNECTION' : 'READY TO BEGIN';\n}\nfunction renderMastery() {\n  const rows = concepts.map(concept => `<div class=\"mastery-row\"><span>${conceptLabel(concept)}</span><div class=\"mastery-meter\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span style=\"width:${Math.round(mastery[concept] * 100)}%\"></span></div><output aria-label=\"${conceptLabel(concept)} estimated mastery ${Math.round(mastery[concept] * 100)} percent\">${Math.round(mastery[concept] * 100)}%</output></div>`).join('');\n  return `<div class=\"mastery-box\"><h3>Estimated mastery · model state, not a grade</h3>${rows}</div>`;\n}\nfunction conceptLabel(concept) { return ({'photosynthesis':'Light capture','glucose':'Stored sugar','cellular-respiration':'Respiration','atp':'ATP transfer'})[concept] ?? concept; }\nfunction renderQuestion() {\n  const item = selectNextItem(deck.items, asked, mastery);\n  if (!item) return renderResults();\n  asked.add(item.id);\n  root.innerHTML = `<article class=\"question-card\"><div class=\"question-type\">CONNECTION ${String(answers.length + 1).padStart(2, '0')} · ${conceptLabel(item.concept).toUpperCase()}</div><h2>${item.prompt}</h2><p class=\"prompt\">Choose the best explanation, then connect it to the larger idea.</p><div class=\"choices\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Answer options\">${item.options.map((option, i) => `<button class=\"choice\" data-choice=\"${i}\"><span class=\"choice-key\">${String.fromCharCode(65+i)}</span>${option}</button>`).join('')}</div><div id=\"feedback-slot\"></div>${renderMastery()}</article>`;\n  root.querySelectorAll('[data-choice]').forEach(button => button.addEventListener('click', () => submitAnswer(item, Number(button.dataset.choice))));\n  const first = root.querySelector('[data-choice]');\n  first?.focus({preventScroll:true});\n  setProgress();\n}\nfunction submitAnswer(item, choice) {\n  if (answers.some(answer => answer.item === item.id)) return;\n  const correct = choice === item.answer;\n  answers.push(Object.freeze({item: item.id, concept:item.concept, choice, correct}));\n  mastery[item.concept] = updateMastery(mastery[item.concept] ?? DEFAULT_BKT.initial, correct);\n  root.querySelectorAll('[data-choice]').forEach(button => {\n    button.disabled = true;\n    const selected = Number(button.dataset.choice);\n    if (selected === item.answer) button.classList.add('correct');\n    else if (selected === choice) button.classList.add('incorrect');\n  });\n  const feedback = document.querySelector('#feedback-slot');\n  feedback.innerHTML = `<div class=\"feedback\"><strong>${correct ? 'That connection holds.' : 'Here’s the correction.'}</strong> ${item.explanation}<span class=\"why\">Try this transfer: ${item.transfer}</span></div><button class=\"primary-button next-button\" id=\"next-button\">${answers.length === deck.items.length ? 'See your learning trace' : 'Follow the next thread'} <span aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></button>`;\n  feedback.querySelector('button').addEventListener('click', renderQuestion);\n  const state = root.querySelector('.mastery-box');\n  state.outerHTML = renderMastery();\n  setProgress();\n  feedback.querySelector('button').focus();\n}\nfunction renderResults() {\n  inPractice = false;\n  review ??= createReview(deck.items, answers);\n  const correct = answers.filter(answer => answer.correct).length;\n  root.innerHTML = `<article class=\"result-card\"><div class=\"result-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⌁</div><div class=\"card-kicker\">YOUR LEARNING TRACE</div><h2 tabindex=\"-1\">Notice the links you built.</h2><p id=\"first-try-summary\">You made ${correct} of ${answers.length} connections on the first try. That count is a snapshot—not a measure of your ability. Review the concept estimates and choose one connection to explain in your own words.</p>${renderMastery()}${renderPracticeSummary()}<section class=\"review-list\" aria-labelledby=\"review-title\"><h3 id=\"review-title\">Review the connections</h3><p>Open a question to see your first answer, the explanation, and an idea to apply.</p>${review.map(renderReviewItem).join('')}</section><div class=\"reflection\"><strong>Apply it:</strong> Trace energy from sunlight to a cell doing work. Where does the form of energy change, and what molecule transfers it to cellular processes?</div><p class=\"source-note\"><strong>Demo deck attribution:</strong> Original question wording adapted from OpenStax, <cite>Biology 2e</cite>, Chapters 7–8, Rice University, CC BY 4.0. ${deck.attribution}</p><button class=\"reset-button\" id=\"reset-button\">Start a fresh local session</button></article>`;\n  root.querySelector('#reset-button').addEventListener('click', () => location.reload());\n  root.querySelector('#practice-button')?.addEventListener('click', () => {\n    practice ??= beginPractice(review);\n    inPractice = true;\n    renderPracticeQuestion();\n  });\n  setProgress();\n  root.querySelector('h2').focus();\n}\n\nfunction escapeHtml(value) {\n  return String(value).replace(/[&<>\"']/g, char => ({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','\"':'&quot;',\"'\":'&#39;'})[char]);\n}\n\nfunction renderReviewItem(item, index) {\n  const retry = practice?.answers.find(answer => answer.item === item.id);\n  const practiceAnswer = retry\n    ? `<div class=\"review-practice-answer\"><strong>Practice answer · ${retry.correct ? 'correct on retry' : 'keep reviewing'}</strong><p>${escapeHtml(item.options[retry.choice])}</p></div>`\n    : '';\n  return `<details class=\"review-item\"><summary><span class=\"review-label\">${String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')} · ${escapeHtml(conceptLabel(item.concept))}</span><span class=\"review-status ${item.correct ? 'is-correct' : 'needs-review'}\">${item.correct ? 'Correct' : 'Needs review'} · first try</span><span class=\"review-prompt\">${escapeHtml(item.prompt)}</span></summary><div class=\"review-body\"><dl class=\"review-answers\"><div><dt>Your first answer</dt><dd>${escapeHtml(item.options[item.choice])}</dd></div><div><dt>Correct answer</dt><dd>${escapeHtml(item.options[item.answer])}</dd></div></dl><p>${escapeHtml(item.explanation)}</p><p class=\"review-transfer\"><strong>Apply the idea:</strong> ${escapeHtml(item.transfer)}</p>${practiceAnswer}</div></details>`;\n}\n\nfunction renderPracticeSummary() {\n  const missed = review.filter(item => !item.correct).length;\n  if (!missed) return '<section class=\"practice-summary\"><h3>Every connection held on the first try.</h3><p>Revisit any explanation below, then try applying the energy pathway in your own words.</p></section>';\n  const total = practice?.items.length ?? missed;\n  const count = practice?.answers.length ?? 0;\n  const complete = practice && count === total;\n  const status = complete\n    ? `You answered ${practice.answers.filter(answer => answer.correct).length} of ${total} correctly on retry.`\n    : practice ? `Practice paused: ${count} of ${total} answered. Resume with the next unanswered prompt.`\n    : `Try ${missed === 1 ? 'the missed connection' : `each of the ${missed} missed connections`} once more.`;\n  const button = complete ? '' : `<button class=\"primary-button\" id=\"practice-button\">${practice ? 'Resume practice' : `Practice ${missed} missed ${missed === 1 ? 'connection' : 'connections'}`} <span aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></button>`;\n  return `<section class=\"practice-summary\" aria-labelledby=\"practice-title\"><h3 id=\"practice-title\">${complete ? 'Practice complete' : 'A second pass'}</h3><p id=\"practice-status\">${status}</p><p class=\"practice-note\">You have seen these explanations. Practice is for recall; your first answers and model estimates stay as they were.</p>${button}</section>`;\n}\n\nfunction renderPracticeQuestion() {\n  const item = currentPracticeItem(practice);\n  if (!item) return renderResults();\n  const number = practice.answers.length + 1;\n  root.innerHTML = `<article class=\"question-card practice-card\"><div class=\"question-type\">PRACTICE ${number} OF ${practice.items.length} · ${escapeHtml(conceptLabel(item.concept).toUpperCase())}</div><h2>${escapeHtml(item.prompt)}</h2><p class=\"prompt\">Recall the explanation. Choose the best answer once more.</p><p class=\"practice-note\">This retry is recorded separately from your first try and model estimates.</p><div class=\"choices\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Practice answer options\">${item.options.map((option, i) => `<button class=\"choice\" data-practice-choice=\"${i}\"><span class=\"choice-key\">${String.fromCharCode(65+i)}</span>${escapeHtml(option)}</button>`).join('')}</div><div id=\"practice-feedback\"></div><button class=\"text-button practice-back\" id=\"back-to-review\">Back to learning trace</button></article>`;\n  let submitted = false;\n  root.querySelectorAll('[data-practice-choice]').forEach(button => button.addEventListener('click', () => {\n    if (submitted) return;\n    const choice = Number(button.dataset.practiceChoice);\n    practice = answerPractice(practice, item.id, choice);\n    submitted = true;\n    const correct = choice === item.answer;\n    root.querySelectorAll('[data-practice-choice]').forEach(option => {\n      option.disabled = true;\n      const selected = Number(option.dataset.practiceChoice);\n      if (selected === item.answer) option.classList.add('correct');\n      else if (selected === choice) option.classList.add('incorrect');\n    });\n    const feedback = root.querySelector('#practice-feedback');\n    feedback.innerHTML = `<div class=\"feedback\"><strong>${correct ? 'That connection holds on retry.' : 'Keep this connection in view.'}</strong><p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> ${escapeHtml(item.options[item.answer])}</p>${escapeHtml(item.explanation)}<span class=\"why\">Apply the idea: ${escapeHtml(item.transfer)}</span></div><button class=\"primary-button next-button\" id=\"practice-next\">${currentPracticeItem(practice) ? 'Next practice connection' : 'See practice results'} <span aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></button>`;\n    feedback.querySelector('button').addEventListener('click', renderPracticeQuestion);\n    setProgress();\n    feedback.querySelector('button').focus();\n  }));\n  root.querySelector('#back-to-review').addEventListener('click', renderResults);\n  setProgress();\n  root.querySelector('[data-practice-choice]').focus({preventScroll:true});\n}\n\ndocument.querySelector('#simulation-button').addEventListener('click', () => {\n  const truth = { photosynthesis:0.82, glucose:0.66, 'cellular-respiration':0.53, atp:0.74 };\n  const adaptive = runLearnerSimulation(deck.items, concepts, truth, true, 41);\n  const fixed = runLearnerSimulation(deck.items, concepts, truth, false, 41);\n  const correctCount = run => run.trace.filter(answer => answer.correct).length;\n  document.querySelector('#simulation-output').innerHTML = `<div class=\"simulation-result\"><strong>Scripted run · not learner evidence</strong><p>One deterministic toy profile (latent concept strengths: 0.53–0.82), same seed and response model; only item order changes.</p><div class=\"simulation-compare\"><span>Adaptive: ${correctCount(adaptive)}/${adaptive.trace.length} correct · mean estimated mastery ${Math.round(adaptive.meanEstimatedMastery * 100)}%</span><span>Fixed order: ${correctCount(fixed)}/${fixed.trace.length} correct · mean estimated mastery ${Math.round(fixed.meanEstimatedMastery * 100)}%</span></div><p>One tiny synthetic run is not a performance claim. Use the test suite for invariants; efficacy needs a larger, preregistered study.</p></div>`;\n});\ndocument.querySelector('#start-button').addEventListener('click', renderQuestion);\nsetProgress();\n\n</script>\n\n\n</body>",
    "text": "Skip to lesson\nr\nrecall\nweave\n● PRIVATE · RUNS ON THIS DEVICE\nFIELD NOTES / BIOLOGY 01\nLearn the links,\nnot just the labels.\n\nBuild a mental model of how cells move energy—from sunlight to usable work. Each next prompt is chosen from your learning state, not a fixed quiz order.\n\n↗\n6 short challenges\n·\nabout 4 minutes\n01\nLight energy\n02\nGlucose\n03\nATP\n\nA concept pathway. The selector can revisit a prerequisite when evidence suggests a useful review.\n\nPRACTICE ROUND\n1 / 6\nPRACTICE 1 OF 6 · LIGHT CAPTURE\nA plant is kept in light but cannot make a key photosynthetic pigment. Which first-order change is most likely?\n\nRecall the explanation. Choose the best answer once more.\n\nThis retry is recorded separately from your first try and model estimates.\n\nALess light energy is captured for the light reactions\nBMore ATP is made directly from stored glucose\nCCarbon dioxide becomes the main energy source\nDOxygen is consumed to split water\nKeep this connection in view.\n\nCorrect answer: Less light energy is captured for the light reactions\n\nPhotosynthetic pigments absorb light energy that drives the light-dependent reactions. Blocking pigment function reduces that energy capture.\nApply the idea: This links a visible input (light) to the first energy-conversion step.\nNext practice connection →\nBack to learning trace\nHow the adaptive model works\nRECALLWEAVE · DEMO BUILD\nOriginal demo deck · OpenStax Biology 2e attribution in the wrap-up"
  }
}
